1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,371
Here's a file.

2
00:00:02,717 --> 00:00:13,256
It feels like a thing. Something you can pick
up and hand to a friend. But a file isn't a
thing. It's a sequence of bytes, and a format
is an agreement about how to read them.

3
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:21,339
Read those bytes under the right agreement,
and you get a photograph. Read the same bytes
under the wrong one, and you get noise.

4
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:32,335
So a conversion is not a transformation of the
thing itself. It's a translation between
agreements. Decode one. Hold the meaning in
the middle. Encode the next.

5
00:00:32,983 --> 00:00:49,501
The middle is where the work happens. A PNG
and a WebP disagree about compression,
headers, almost everything. But they agree on
what a pixel is. A CSV and a JSON file
disagree about punctuation. They agree on what
a row is.

6
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:53,341
Morph is a small machine built around that
middle.

7
00:00:53,983 --> 00:01:13,851
Drop in a PNG, and the browser decodes it into
pixels. The canvas paints those pixels, then
encodes them again as WebP. WebP can spend
fewer bytes on detail your eye is unlikely to
miss. That's where most of the ninety eight
percent goes. Same image, new agreement, and
it never left this tab.

8
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:30,467
Or take a CSV. Rows in, named objects out. The
header becomes keys. Quoted commas stay inside
their fields. Numbers stop pretending to be
text. The JSON is bigger, and that's fine,
because it carries its own labels now.

9
00:01:31,167 --> 00:01:39,611
Not every conversion is real. PDF output is a
labelled demo, a valid file with a clear
badge, not a fake success.

10
00:01:40,350 --> 00:01:51,754
The conversion needs no server. The browser
already carries the translators. Image
decoders, text parsers, encoders built into
the canvas. Morph simply conducts them.

11
00:01:52,450 --> 00:02:00,119
So the file never has to leave. No upload bar,
no queue, no account. The work happens on the
machine in front of you.

12
00:02:00,817 --> 00:02:10,322
That's the quiet part. A file converter used
to mean someone else's computer. Now it can be
a tab, a canvas, and a small act of chemistry.

13
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:16,863
A file was never the thing. It was the
agreement. Morph lets you watch the agreement
change.

14
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:20,871
It's live on GitHub Pages. Bring a file.
